lessons.body:
Every element on a web page is a rectangular box. Understanding the layers of that box explains most layout puzzles.

## The four layers

From the inside out, each box has:

- Content, which is the text or image itself
- Padding, the space between the content and the border
- Border, a line around the padding
- Margin, the space outside the border that separates the box from its neighbours

    .card {
      padding: 16px;
      border: 2px solid #5fa8d3;
      margin: 24px 0;
    }

With two values, `24px 0` means 24 pixels top and bottom and zero left and right. With four values, the order is top, right, bottom, left, which you can remember as clockwise from the top.

## How big is a box?

By default, width describes only the content, so padding and border are added on top. A box with `width: 300px` and `padding: 20px` ends up 340 pixels wide, which surprises many beginners. Fix this by switching to a friendlier rule that includes padding and border in the width:

    * {
      box-sizing: border-box;
    }

Most developers put this at the top of every stylesheet.

## Margin collapsing and centring

Vertical margins between neighbouring blocks can merge into one, using the larger value rather than adding them. To centre a block horizontally, give it a width and set the left and right margins to auto:

    .container {
      max-width: 720px;
      margin: 0 auto;
    }

> Tip: in developer tools, hover over an element to see a coloured diagram of its margin, border and padding. It makes the model visible.

Practise by turning three paragraphs into bordered cards with padding, spacing between them, and a centred container.

Flexbox is a CSS layout system for arranging items in a row or a column. It replaced older tricks and makes common layouts simple.

## Turning it on

Set `display: flex` on a parent, called the flex container. Its direct children become flex items and line up in a row.

    .menu {
      display: flex;
      gap: 16px;
      justify-content: space-between;
      align-items: center;
    }

Here is what each property does:

- `gap` adds space between items.
- `justify-content` positions items along the main axis, which is horizontal by default. Values include `flex-start`, `center`, `space-between` and `space-around`.
- `align-items` positions items along the cross axis, which is vertical by default.
- `flex-direction: column` switches the main axis to vertical.
- `flex-wrap: wrap` lets items move onto a new line when space runs out.

## Sizing items

The `flex` shorthand on an item controls how it grows. For example, `flex: 1` tells every item to share the free space equally, which gives equal-width columns:

    .cards {
      display: flex;
      gap: 20px;
    }
    .cards .card {
      flex: 1;
    }

## A classic example: centring

To centre something both ways inside a box with a set height:

    .hero {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: center;
      align-items: center;
      min-height: 60vh;
    }

> Tip: remember that flexbox works in one dimension at a time. For full grids of rows and columns, look at CSS Grid later.

Build a navigation bar with a logo on the left and links on the right, and a row of three equal cards below it.

More than half of web visits happen on phones, so every page you build should adapt to small screens. This idea is called responsive design.

## Start with mobile

Make sure your page contains the viewport meta tag from the first lesson. Then write styles for a narrow screen first, because a single column of content is simple and works everywhere. Add enhancements for wider screens afterwards.

## Fluid images

Prevent images from overflowing their container with:

    img {
      max-width: 100%;
      height: auto;
    }

## Media queries

A media query applies styles only when a condition is true. This one switches a column of cards into a row on screens at least 700 pixels wide:

    .cards {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 16px;
    }

    @media (min-width: 700px) {
      .cards {
        flex-direction: row;
      }
    }

Choose breakpoints where your content starts to look awkward, not to match particular devices. Drag the browser window narrower and wider and notice when the design needs help.

## Testing

Use the device toolbar in your browser's developer tools to preview phone and tablet sizes. Also check that you can reach every link using only the Tab key.

## Publishing

A static site made of HTML, CSS and images can be hosted for free. Popular options include GitHub Pages, Netlify and Cloudflare Pages. You upload or connect your project folder, and the service gives you a public web address. Make sure the main file is named `index.html`, so the server finds it automatically.

> Tip: before publishing, validate your HTML with the free W3C validator and fix any errors it reports.

Finish by making your page work at 360 pixels wide, then publish it.
